Serum Beta-hCG Testing and Early Pregnancy Counselling
General Practice Other
Missed menstrual period, Positive home pregnancy test, Mild early pregnancy symptoms, Absence of severe pelvic pain

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-040
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe emotional distress and panic attack following an unexpected positive home pregnancy test, requiring immediate medical confirmation and urgent counselling.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a highly distressed patient who experienced a severe panic attack upon reading a positive home pregnancy test this evening. The patient is experiencing severe anxiety, hyperventilation, and is entirely unable to cope with the unexpected result. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to physically manage the acute autonomic hyperarousal, rule out an ectopic emergency via history and examination, perform a point-of-care test for confirmation, and provide immediate, non-judgmental early pregnancy counselling to stabilize the psychiatric crisis. Delaying assessment would have left the patient in a state of unmanageable distress overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My period was late, the stick just turned positive, and my heart is racing so fast I can't catch my breath. I am terrified."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents as a female presenting with a delayed menstrual period (currently 6 days late). Following a positive home urine pregnancy test one hour ago, she experienced a severe acute stress reaction. The patient reports mild early pregnancy symptoms such as morning nausea and breast tenderness over the last week. There is a complete absence of severe unilateral pelvic pain or heavy vaginal bleeding, ruling out an immediate surgical emergency. The presentation mandates a careful discussion of the patient's intentions regarding the unexpected pregnancy, as the patient is currently overwhelmed.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently exhibiting high psychomotor agitation, pacing, and crying.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe situational anxiety; patient is living alone and lacks immediate emotional support tonight.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/88 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Tearful, anxious, visibly trembling, holding the plastic pregnancy test.
Physical Exam: Abdominal: Soft, completely non-tender in all four quadrants. No palpable masses. Pelvic: (External only) No active vaginal bleeding reported or observed. The clinical presentation required immediate psychiatric de-escalation and addressing the patient anxiety. A point-of-care urine beta-hCG was performed, confirming the positive result. The encounter lacks acute haemodynamic instability. Expected investigation results include a quantitative serum beta-hCG appropriately elevated for the estimated gestational age and an intrauterine gestational sac visible on early dating ultrasound to rule out an ectopic location.
Environmental Observations: Safe, private apartment; highly suitable for a confidential reproductive health discussion.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Early Pregnancy Counselling (Complicated by Acute Situational Panic) - Status: Stable (Physiologically)
Differentials:

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of unilateral lower abdominal pain, absence of shoulder tip pain, absence of vaginal bleeding, and stable blood pressure. (Requires formal ultrasound for definitive exclusion).

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The tachycardia and tachypnea are entirely congruent with a severe panic attack triggered by life-altering news, resolving with verbal de-escalation.

False Positive Urine Test - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Highly accurate modern tests, especially in the presence of an overdue period and classic early pregnancy symptoms (nausea, breast tenderness).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High emotional distress)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe acute stress reaction triggered by an unexpected positive pregnancy test, mimicking an acute physiological crisis. Telephone triage accurately identified the necessity for immediate medical assessment to rule out an ectopic rupture and to manage the severe panic. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was mandatory to physically examine the abdomen, definitively rule out signs of acute internal bleeding, confirm the diagnosis, and provide immediate, objective, non-judgmental counselling. Providing a clear, step-by-step medical plan completely neutralized the paralyzing anxiety of the unknown, preventing an unwarranted ED presentation for acute panic tonight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a physical examination confirming absolute abdominal stability. Provided extensive verbal de-escalation and non-judgmental counselling regarding all available reproductive options. Calculated the estimated date of delivery based on the last menstrual period (approx. 5 weeks gestation). Generated urgent pathology requests for quantitative serum Beta-hCG, blood group, and a formal early dating ultrasound.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the overwhelming anxiety regarding this decision, commit to a strict "no internet research" rule for the next 48 hours. Place the pathology forms in an envelope on your fridge. By explicitly delaying any further action or research until you have the formal blood test results and a clear mind tomorrow, you actively shut down the immediate panic spiral, giving your brain the necessary space to process the shock calmly.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 48 hours to review the formal serum hCG results and discuss the patient's intentions moving forward.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop sudden, severe, doubling-over pain in your lower abdomen, if you experience pain referring to the tip of your shoulder, or if you begin bleeding heavily.

